# Carrier Wound #26: The Unbounded Normalize Grind Rode To A Blind SIGKILL

Wound #25 (the recovery copy aborted the lane blind) shipped and we re-drove a
dream run watching it node-by-node over a live `wrangler tail` — not staring at a
status page, *reading the worker*. The lane did NOT block at a step with a named
cause this time. It went **further** and died worse: the worker logged

```
Command timeout after 780000ms (872879ms)
```

— the OUTER server-side `sandbox.exec` ceiling SIGKILLed the whole lane after it
ran **872 seconds**, past the 750s MIDDLE shell `timeout` that was supposed to reap
it first. And the recoverable MIDDLE SIGTERM left **no failure marker at all**: the
last heartbeat line was `normalize-output`, frozen, written 413s after pi-invoke
started. pi had finished. normalize-output started and never returned. The planner
got a blind *"no result marker"* — no step cause, no stderr, nothing. The exact
failure shape the whole week's work exists to kill, one layer deeper than #25.

## Mechanism — the one heavy post-pi step had no inner time bound

The lane runs three nested timeouts (`cloudflare-sandbox-agent-lanes.ts`):

- **OUTER** = `sandbox.exec`'s hard ceiling = `input.input.timeoutMs` (780,000ms
  observed). On expiry the SDK throws and **discards the command's stdout** — so
  anything not already flushed to a captured channel is gone.
- **MIDDLE** = the whole-script `timeout "$PIWF_COMMAND_TIMEOUT_SECONDS" bash`
  (=750s), decoupled BELOW the OUTER by the reaper guard so its SIGTERM fires first
  and runs the INT/TERM trap → `emit_failure_marker` BEFORE the OUTER SIGKILL.
- **INNER** = a per-step `timeout`. Wound #22-B gave **pi-invoke** one
  (`timeout "$pi_budget_s" pi`). **normalize-output had none.**

normalize-output was a bare `node <<'NODE' … NODE` — the ONE heavy post-pi step
with no inner bound. On a pathological pi output (oversized or truncated event
stream) the node normalizer grinds for minutes. Not on CPU — the O(n)
`scanBudget = raw.length * 4 + 1_000_000` in `extractFirstJsonValueText` bounds
*iterations*, and even tens of MB scan in seconds. It grinds on **memory**:
`readFileSync` of the whole raw output + `raw.split("\n")` into a giant array +
slices, on the memory-starved `lite` instance → GC thrash / swap. The line-22
comment in `agent-lane-json-output.ts` literally predicted it: *"grinds for minutes
on the lite sandbox … under the memory pressure that grind creates."*

Here is the part that made it **blind** instead of merely slow. The MIDDLE
`timeout` sent SIGTERM to bash at 750s. **bash cannot run a trap while it is
blocked on a busy FOREGROUND `node`.** The pending SIGTERM sat unserviced — the
shell was parked in `wait()` on a child pegging the event loop — until the OUTER
780s SIGKILL erased bash *and* node together. SIGKILL runs no trap. So:
`emit_failure_marker` never fired, no heartbeat-derived cause reached stdout, and
the stdout that might have carried something was discarded by the OUTER throw. A
slow step with no inner bound doesn't just take long — it **denies the supervisor
its own reaping window** and converts a recoverable SIGTERM into an unrecoverable
SIGKILL.

pi-invoke survives this class because it self-bounds: `timeout "$pi_budget_s" pi`
returns control to bash *before* the MIDDLE timeout, so the trap can run.
normalize-output was its un-bounded twin — exactly the wound #22-B shape, one step
to the right.

> Release It! (Michael Nygard), "Timeouts" / Ch. 4, Stability Antipatterns:
>
> > Networks failures can hit you in two ways: fast or slow. … Slow failures, such
> > as a dropped ACK, let threads block for minutes before throwing exceptions. The
> > blocked thread can't process other transactions, so overall capacity is
> > reduced. … Clearly, a slow response is a lot worse than no response.

A blocking call you do not bound yourself will block for as long as something below
you allows — here, until the OUTER kill. *A slow response is a lot worse than no
response*: a fast NAMED failure the receipt can commit beats a silent grind that
rides to a blind SIGKILL. Bound every blocking call at the level that owns it.

## Fix — self-bound normalize-output exactly like pi-invoke

`cloudflare-sandbox-agent-lane-command.ts`. The bare `node` becomes a bounded,
attributable step, mirroring the pi-invoke pattern byte-for-byte:

```bash
rm -f "$LANE_OUTPUT_NORMALIZATION_PATH" 2>>"$stderr_path" || true
normalize_elapsed_s=$(( $(date +%s) - script_start_s ))
normalize_budget_s=$(( PIWF_COMMAND_TIMEOUT_SECONDS - normalize_elapsed_s - PIWF_NORMALIZE_TAIL_MARGIN_SECONDS ))
if [ "$normalize_budget_s" -gt "$PIWF_NORMALIZE_MAX_SECONDS" ]; then
  normalize_budget_s="$PIWF_NORMALIZE_MAX_SECONDS"
fi
if [ "$normalize_budget_s" -lt 1 ]; then
  normalize_budget_s=1
fi
set +e
timeout -s KILL "$normalize_budget_s" node <<'NODE'
${jsonOutputNormalizerNodeScript}
NODE
normalize_status=$?
set -e
```

Three deliberate choices:

1. **A small fixed cap, not "all remaining budget."** A real verdict normalizes in
   well under a second. When pi finishes early (it ran 413s of 750s) the remaining
   budget is ~322s — *exactly the grind window to avoid*. So the budget is
   `min(remaining, PIWF_NORMALIZE_MAX_SECONDS=60)`, clamped ≥1. 60s is pure safety
   margin, not a work budget; a healthy normalize never approaches it.

2. **`-s KILL`, not the default TERM.** A node wedged in a synchronous parse/GC
   grind cannot service a TERM between event-loop ticks — the same reason bash
   couldn't service the MIDDLE TERM. SIGKILL reliably stops it.

3. **Synthesize the named outcome when the kill left none.** A `timeout`/OOM
   SIGKILL aborts node BEFORE it records its outcome file, so the file is absent;
   a normal parse failure writes the file with its own reason. The build-receipt
   step DEFAULTS `outputNormalization` to `{ normalized: true }` and only overrides
   if that file exists — so a killed normalize that left no file would forge a
   **clean** receipt over unnormalized output. The shell now stamps the honest
   cause itself, only when node left nothing behind:

```bash
if [ ! -s "$LANE_OUTPUT_NORMALIZATION_PATH" ]; then
  printf '{"normalized":false,"reason":"normalize_timeout",...}\n' \
    > "$LANE_OUTPUT_NORMALIZATION_PATH" 2>>"$stderr_path" || true
  printf '[normalize-output self-bound] node normalizer exceeded its %ss budget…\n' \
    "$normalize_budget_s" >> "$stderr_path" 2>>"$stderr_path" || true
fi
persist_unnormalized_output
```

So a slow/killed normalize now commits `status:"failed"` with
`outputNormalization.reason: "normalize_timeout"` and a stderr line naming
normalize-output as the wedge — the next re-drive decision is made on a named
cause, not a blind SIGKILL. Two env vars carry the bound (`cloudflare-sandbox-agent-lanes.ts`):
`PIWF_NORMALIZE_MAX_SECONDS=60`, `PIWF_NORMALIZE_TAIL_MARGIN_SECONDS=20`.

## Chaos Receipt (would have caught it)

`tests/integration/cloudflare-sandbox-agent-lanes.test.ts` — **414 passing** (2
new, +1 hardened). Both new tests are hostile doubles in the file's established
style:

- **Shape** — asserts the real command string carries the `timeout -s KILL
  "$normalize_budget_s" node` wrapper, the elapsed/cap/clamp budget arithmetic, the
  pre-run `rm -f` of the outcome file, and the `normalize_timeout` stamp. Fails
  against the pre-fix bare `node`.
- **Hostile double** — slices the REAL normalize-output block out of
  `buildPiAgentLaneCommand()` and runs it verbatim under production's `set -eu`
  with a `node` that NEVER returns (`sleep 20`, far past the 2s test budget) and
  writes no outcome — exactly an OOM/timeout SIGKILL. It asserts the grind is
  **bounded to ~2s** (not the full 20s), the outcome is the NAMED
  `reason:"normalize_timeout"`, `LANE_OUTPUT_PATH` is left for the downstream
  steps, and the wedge is attributed to normalize-output in captured stderr.
  Against the pre-fix unbounded `node` it runs the full sleep, leaves no outcome,
  and goes blind. A polite test would have fed a small parseable blob that
  normalizes instantly; this one constructs the multi-minute grind the production
  lane actually got into.

The #25 hostile double was hardened to supply the production normalize env
(`LANE_OUTPUT_NORMALIZATION_PATH`, the budget vars, `script_start_s`) and to make
its fake node record its outcome before exiting — faithful to production, so the
two wounds stay cleanly separated (a failing *copy* vs a killed *normalize*).

## Verification

```bash
pnpm vitest run tests/integration/cloudflare-sandbox-agent-lanes.test.ts  # 33 pass (2 new)
pnpm vitest run                                                            # 414 pass, 55 files
pnpm typecheck   # clean (tsgo)
pnpm check       # oxfmt + oxlint + type-aware, clean
```

## Known follow-ups (not in this fix)

- **Input-size fast-cap.** An O(1) `if (raw.length > CAP) throw OVERSIZED` would
  reject a giant blob in milliseconds instead of after a (now-bounded) 60s grind.
  Deferred until a live run yields a real raw-output size to calibrate CAP without
  rejecting legit large event streams; the `timeout` bound is mechanism-agnostic
  and sufficient now.
- **Deterministic re-drive should fail fast.** Inherited from #25 — a planner that
  fails deterministically (now with a named cause) should not be re-driven for
  generations. Fold into the pause/re-arm seam.
- **`adapter_unavailable` overload.** Still conflates a planner-no-plan with a
  transport outage. Reclassify alongside the backpressure pause work.
- **The deeper question this now lets us READ:** *why* did this run's normalize
  grind — is pi emitting an oversized event stream, or a truncated one? The next
  live run's committed `reason` (`normalize_timeout` vs `oversized_output` vs
  `no_parseable_output`) names it. Diagnose from the reason — do not guess.

## Burn-In Gate

Per the Phase 2 D3 acceptance bar, the 10-run count stays at **zero**. This fix
deploys, then ONE dream-shape run is watched node-by-node over `wrangler tail` —
either to `captured` (pi produced a real blueprint, the carrier carried it) or to
an **honest, named** block that tells us the next carrier move without staring.
Only a `captured` on fixed, deployed code fires the operator Discord ping with the
wzrrd HITL URL, resets D3, and starts the serial burn-in. Sibling of
[[carrier-wound-recovery-cp-blind-abort-2026-06-14]]: #25 made the recovery copy
stop killing the lane in silence; #26 makes the one heavy step that had no clock of
its own stop riding past the supervisor into a blind kill. *Any failure is a
carrier failure: fix the class in the carrier, with a chaos test that would have
caught it, reset the count.*
